```markdown
## Android additions
- Follow the established Kotlin, Compose, ViewModel, StateFlow, coroutine, Room,
  Retrofit/approved HTTP, and Hilt conventions in the application.
- Compose renders state and emits actions; ViewModels coordinate use cases and
  expose read-only state. Do not access Retrofit or Room directly from UI code.
- Keep domain models and repository contracts independent of Android UI and concrete
  transport/storage APIs. Use suspend/lifecycle-aware operations and propagate
  coroutine cancellation instead of converting it into an ordinary failure.
- Keep account-scoped persistence, migrations, and session access checks explicit.
- Preserve the approved JDK/Gradle/AGP/Kotlin/Compose compatibility set. Do not copy
  a plugin version or API import from another project without checking compatibility.
- Use the actual unit-test and JUnit/Espresso instrumentation targets. Reuse real
  resource IDs, screen objects, idling resources, and the configured device matrix.
- Use the repository's single-responsibility file conventions and approved SDK APIs.
```

Reference: [Android Standards](/mobile/platforms/native-android-standards/).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

```markdown
## iOS additions
- Follow the application's Swift, SwiftUI/UIKit, observable state, and repository
  conventions. Keep UI state and its ViewModel appropriately main-actor isolated.
- Keep domain values independent of SwiftUI/UIKit, concrete URLSession adapters,
  and persistence implementations. Pass immutable Sendable values across actors.
- Keep SwiftData contexts/models inside their owning isolation boundary. Return
  domain snapshots rather than leaking persistence objects to presentation.
- Distinguish compiler version, language mode, deployment target, and default actor
  isolation. Do not copy the reference example's minimum OS into an existing app
  or weaken concurrency checking without an approved compatibility decision.
- Use structured task ownership and cancellation; guard against stale account or
  request results after suspension. An actor alone does not prevent reentrancy bugs.
- Preserve the actual unit/XCUITest targets, accessibility identifiers, and bounded
  condition waits. Do not replace synchronization with sleeps.
- Keep each class, struct, and protocol in its own single-responsibility file as
  required by the platform convention. Preserve documented signing ownership.
```

Reference: [iOS Standards](/mobile/platforms/native-ios-standards/).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

```markdown
## React Native additions
- React Native is strongly preferred for new apps; preserve an existing app's
  supported stack unless migration is explicitly agreed.
- Use strict TypeScript and validate external values at runtime. Do not use any,
  broad type assertions, or disabled checks to conceal an incompatible contract.
- Use Redux Toolkit for shared application state. Keep reducers pure and state
  serializable; inject clients and use cases outside Redux state.
- Keep local UI state local when appropriate. Preserve the existing server-data
  policy; do not duplicate the same data across RTK Query and a competing cache.
- Views/hooks coordinate through feature contracts, not concrete HTTP or storage
  instances. Handle cancellation, request identity, and account-session changes.
- Preserve the approved native-module/toolchain compatibility and selected lockfile.
  Rebuild native apps when native dependencies change; Metro reload is insufficient.
- Keep JavaScript feature/store tests in the existing JS runner. Preserve the team's
  Android JUnit native device-test path; do not replace it with Appium/WebdriverIO.
  Use the approved iOS native UI test setup for iOS device coverage.
- Follow declaration-named, single-responsibility file conventions. Preserve approved
  credential storage, account-scoped persistence, and native integration boundaries.
```

Reference: [React Native Standards](/mobile/platforms/react-native-standards/). Apply the relevant native additions when changing Android or iOS integrations.

